  "narrative": "I assessed all newly returned discovery evidence against the pre-registered claim direction and the 0.15 absolute composite dependency-score threshold. Returned estimates are already signed to the hypothesis direction: for direction -1, a positive signed estimate means the exposed group has a lower composite dependency score, i.e. stronger dependency; for direction +1, a positive signed estimate means a higher composite score, i.e. weaker dependency.\n\nH161 is accepted and closed: in the fully clean genomic/MSI background (TP53, KEAP1, SMARCA4, MSI, MTAP, and CDKN2A wildtype/reference states), PTEN loss has signed estimate +0.2901 with 95% interval [0.1909, 0.3893], entirely above the 0.15-unit threshold in the claimed stronger-dependency direction. This is the strongest interval-level PTEN context evidence so far and argues against the signal being driven by overlap with TP53/KEAP1/SMARCA4/MTAP/CDKN2A or MSI co-states. H162 is accepted and closed: TP53 loss remains associated with weaker dependency among SMARCA4-loss samples, signed +0.8973 [0.7892, 1.0054], far above threshold.\n\nThe high-screen-quality robustness tests are strong for the three large main-effect predictors. H165 accepts TP53 loss under higher screen ROC quality, signed +0.8379 [0.7530, 0.9229]. H166 accepts KEAP1 loss under the same condition, signed +0.7724 [0.6784, 0.8664]. H167 accepts SMARCA4 loss under the same condition, signed +0.8057 [0.7220, 0.8893]. These intervals are clearly above threshold and reduce concern that the accepted TP53, KEAP1, or SMARCA4 associations are artifacts of lower-quality screens, though they remain supplied-dataset associations.\n\nH168 accepts MSI-high within the high mutation-burden subset, signed +0.7450 [0.5826, 0.9073]. Together with earlier evidence that MSI-high persists outside the high mutation-burden tail and in several independent contexts, this supports a broad and robust MSI-high association rather than one tied specifically to high mutation burden.\n\nSeveral PTEN context/robustness tests remain unresolved because their intervals do not provide interval-level evidence exceeding the threshold. H163 (PTEN loss among TP53-loss samples) has signed +0.1601 but interval [-0.0233, 0.3435]; only 54 exposed observations are available, so I close it unresolved and do not interpret it as a reliable context-specific PTEN effect. H164 (PTEN loss under high screen quality) has signed +0.2195 with lower bound +0.0873, below the 0.15 threshold; I keep it active because the point estimate is supportive and a cleaner background/high-quality-screen test is registered. H169 (PTEN loss in adherent culture) has signed +0.2537, but the lower bound +0.1467 just misses the 0.15 threshold. It is supportive of the accepted PTEN association but not decisive as a standalone threshold claim, so I keep it active while registering a cleaner adherent-context refinement.\n\nThe research-signature modifier tests are weak. H156 and H157 are rejected as threshold-level interactions because their signed upper bounds are below 0.15 and the intervals do not support modification in the claimed direction. H158 remains unresolved but closed: the point estimate is +0.0279 and the interval [-0.1208, 0.1767] includes zero, although the upper bound barely reaches threshold; this is too weak and imprecise to support a meaningful KEAP1-by-signature-B interaction. Overall, the new evidence supports the same small robust set of associations: TP53 loss with weaker dependency, KEAP1 loss with weaker dependency, SMARCA4 loss with stronger dependency, MSI-high with very strong dependency, and PTEN loss with stronger dependency in clean and context-restricted analyses. No independent validation can be requested because no voluntary validation requests remain, so all conclusions remain discovery-level associations in the supplied dataset, not causal or externally generalizable claims.",
  "research_notes": "Iteration 13 appraise update. H161 is a key PTEN result: in the fully clean TP53/KEAP1/SMARCA4/MSI/MTAP/CDKN2A background, PTEN loss has +0.290 [0.191, 0.389], entirely above the 0.15 threshold; accepted and closed. H162 accepts TP53 loss within SMARCA4-loss samples (+0.897 [0.789, 1.005]). H165-H167 accept high-screen-ROC robustness for TP53, KEAP1, and SMARCA4 with large intervals well above threshold. H168 accepts MSI-high within high mutation burden (+0.745 [0.583, 0.907]). PTEN robustness is still interval-bound: TP53-loss co-state H163 is unresolved due wide interval [-0.023, 0.344] and only 54 exposed; high screen quality H164 is active because +0.219 [0.087, 0.352] misses threshold lower bound; adherent culture H169 is active because +0.254 [0.147, 0.361] just misses threshold. Registered two targeted PTEN refinements: clean+adherent and clean+high screen quality. Signature modifiers are not compelling: H156 and H157 rejected because upper bounds are below threshold; H158 unresolved closed due near-zero point and imprecision. No validation requests remain, so all conclusions are supplied-dataset associations. Continue to avoid arbitrary CDKN2A context cuts and to treat PTEN as context-associated rather than uniformly causal."
